link2symlink: make PROOT_L2S_DIR hold every file, and link a followed link into its group

Without PROOT_L2S_DIR the file behind a faked hard link and its intermediate
sit next to the link, where the guest lists them. A program that hard links
a directory entry by entry (uv installing wheels out of its cache, pnpm,
cp -al) then links those too: the intermediate fails with EPERM, and the
file behind gets a group of its own inside the first. Hermes's installer
ended there ("failed to hardlink file ... .l2s.tags.py0001: Operation not
permitted"). With PROOT_L2S_DIR they live in one directory outside the
guest's and a listing shows the link alone, so the app sets it. Two things
kept that from working:

A name there was "<file name><NNNN>", counted up from 0001 to 0999: the
thousandth __init__.py took 0999 again and the rename replaced the file
that had it, and two PRoot of one rootfs could pick the same free number.
The number is now the file's inode number, which is its own for as long as
the file exists; when a thousand names in a row are taken, link() fails
with EMLINK instead of replacing a file.

linkat(AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW), which Python's os.link() uses, reached the file
behind a faked hard link and moved it as if it were linked for the first
time. The first group's links then reported the old count, and removing
every link left the file behind. Such a source is now one more link of the
group it belongs to.

